    explanation: "Order picking is often the most labour-intensive activity in a warehouse, and estimating how long it takes is essential for staffing, scheduling, and hitting shipping deadlines. This order picking time calculator multiplies the number of items by the average seconds each takes to pick, then converts to minutes, giving a fast estimate of the labour time an order requires.\n\nThis matters because picking can absorb half or more of warehouse labour costs. Knowing the time per order lets managers schedule the right number of pickers, set realistic cut-off times for same-day shipping, and identify orders large enough to warrant batch or zone picking. It also provides a baseline to measure process improvements against, so you can quantify the gain from better slotting, pick paths, or technology.\n\nThe calculation works because picking time scales with the number of line items when each takes a fairly consistent time to locate, retrieve, and confirm. The default of 30 seconds per item is a reasonable starting point, but your real rate depends on warehouse size, storage method, travel distance, and whether pickers use paper, scanners, or voice systems. Measure your own average and enter it for accuracy. Warehouse supervisors, fulfilment teams, and operations analysts use pick time estimates to balance workloads, plan capacity for peak seasons, and build the throughput models that keep orders flowing out the door on schedule.",
