    formulaExplanation: "This safety stock formula multiplies the gap between peak and average daily demand by the supplier lead time in days. The formula works because the risk of a stockout comes from demand running hotter than expected while you wait for replenishment. The difference between maximum and average usage captures how much extra you could consume on a bad day, and multiplying by lead time totals that excess across the whole reorder window you must cover with a buffer.",
    explanation: "Safety stock is the buffer inventory you hold to protect against running out when demand spikes or a delivery runs late. This safety stock calculator uses the classic formula, multiplying the difference between your maximum and average daily usage by the lead time, to find how many extra units you should keep on hand as insurance against stockouts.\n\nThis matters because stockouts cost sales, damage customer trust, and disrupt production, while too much safety stock ties up cash and warehouse space. Getting the buffer right strikes the balance. The calculation focuses on demand variability: the gap between a busy day and an average day tells you how much extra you might consume, and the lead time tells you how long you are exposed to that risk while waiting for a replenishment order to arrive.\n\nThe formula works because the danger window is the lead time, and the worst case within it is demand running at its maximum rather than its average. Multiplying the daily excess by the number of lead-time days totals the extra units needed to survive a demand spike without a stockout. This straightforward method suits many products, though items with highly variable lead times may warrant a more advanced statistical formula. Inventory planners, purchasing managers, and small business owners use safety stock levels to set reorder points, size replenishment orders, and keep shelves stocked through demand swings without drowning in excess inventory.",
