    formula: "density = weight ÷ (length × width × height ÷ 1,728)",
    formulaExplanation: "Freight class density divides the shipment weight by its volume in cubic feet, where volume comes from multiplying the three dimensions in inches and dividing by 1,728, the number of cubic inches in a cubic foot. The formula works because less-than-truckload carriers assign freight class largely on density, since it captures how efficiently a shipment uses trailer space. A higher density in pounds per cubic foot generally maps to a lower, cheaper class, making this figure the starting point for classifying and pricing LTL freight.",
    explanation: "Freight class determines what you pay to ship less-than-truckload freight, and density in pounds per cubic foot is the single most important factor behind it. This freight class calculator multiplies your shipment's length, width, and height, converts the result to cubic feet, and divides the weight by that volume to give the density carriers use as the starting point for assigning a class.\n\nThis matters because the national freight classification system rates commodities from class 50 for dense, durable goods up to class 500 for light, bulky items, and higher classes cost more. For many products, density alone determines the class, so knowing your pounds per cubic foot lets you predict the class, quote accurately, and avoid the reclassification charges that hit when a carrier re-measures a shipment and finds it lighter or bulkier than declared.\n\nThe calculation works because dividing cubic inches by 1,728 converts to the cubic feet that classification tables use, and dividing weight by volume yields the density those tables are built around. Denser freight packs trailers efficiently and earns a lower class, while low-density freight wastes space and climbs the scale. Note that some commodities are classed by factors beyond density, such as handling, stowability, and liability, so density gives the class for density-based items but not every one. Shippers, brokers, and warehouse teams calculate density before booking to estimate class, compare carrier quotes fairly, and package goods to reach a cheaper class where possible.",
