    explanation: "Chargeable weight is the figure air freight carriers actually bill you on, and it is the greater of your shipment's real weight and its volumetric weight. This chargeable weight calculator multiplies your package dimensions in centimetres, divides by the air freight divisor, and compares the result against the actual weight to show both the volumetric weight and the chargeable weight you will pay for.\n\nThis matters because a light but bulky shipment costs an airline the same cargo space as a heavy one, so carriers charge on whichever is higher: mass or volume. Shippers who quote against actual weight alone are frequently surprised when the freight invoice bills a much higher volumetric figure. Calculating chargeable weight up front lets you compare quotes accurately, choose the right packaging, and forecast air freight costs before booking.\n\nThe calculation works because the volumetric divisor, typically 5,000 for international air freight in centimetres and kilograms, encodes an assumed density: every 5,000 cubic centimetres counts as one kilogram. Multiplying the dimensions and dividing by the divisor converts volume into a weight the carrier can compare against the scale weight. Taking the maximum reflects the carrier's need to recover either the payload or the space, whichever is more valuable. Exporters, ecommerce sellers, and freight forwarders rely on chargeable weight to price shipments, decide between air and sea, and right-size cartons so they do not pay for empty air on every kilogram shipped.",
